  planner_response {
    response: "I will view `EquipmentManagement.tsx` lines 1 to 25."
    thinking: "**Analyzing Modal Component**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the `AddEquipmentModal.tsx` component. I\'m focusing on the props it receives, specifically the types related to equipment, tools, sensors, and categories. I\'m now aiming to understand how these props influence the modal\'s behavior and the data it handles.\n\n\n**Refining Category Handling**\n\nI\'ve just added logic to normalize the `categories` prop, handling both `CategoryItem[]` and `string[]`. I\'m employing `useMemo` for this, transforming string categories into full `CategoryItem` objects with default values. Subsequently, I\'m defining initial states for the equipment form using the normalized categories, setting up `name`, `description`, and `category` states.\n\n\n**Defining Equipment State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on initializing the equipment form\'s state variables. I\'m setting states for numerous equipment attributes such as `maintPlant`, `selectedToolMapping`, `selectedSensors`, `manufacturer`, `engineType`, `partNo`, `licensePlate`, `modelNumber`, `benchmark`, `serialNo`, `enginePower`, `cclNumber`, and `fuelCapacity`. I\'m using default values or initial category information where available to populate these states. I have also implemented a `handleCategoryChange` function to update relevant state variables based on the selected category.\n\n\n**Implementing Category-Driven Defaults**\n\nI\'m now implementing logic to dynamically set equipment form defaults based on the selected category. The `useEffect` hook triggers when the modal opens or the category changes. Based on the selected category, I\'m pre-filling fields like `engineType` and `fuelCapacity` with values derived from the category data. I\'ve considered potential edge cases, like the user changing the category and updating the form state accordingly.\n\n\n**Enhancing Auto-Fill Features**\n\nI\'m working on auto-filling equipment form fields based on the selected category. Specifically, I\'m setting `fuelCapacity` to \"0\" for electric motors. I\'m also using `useMemo` to retrieve the active category object, which allows me to display a helpful badge showing the auto-filled specifications right next to the category label. This enhances the user experience and provides clarity about the auto-fill process. I will also display the same values right above the Engine Type field.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view `EquipmentManagement.tsx` lines 1 to 25."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 172477673
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
