input field for instant mapping population. The logic is now centered on the `toolName` change and the subsequent matching. I\'m prioritizing the user experience of matching or clearing the tool mapping, and handling the manual selection mode. I\'m focusing on ensuring data consistency when the user selects a tool name and matching behavior. I\'m confident about making sure the correct sensors and parameters are shown or hidden.\n\n\n**Implementing Matching Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the matching logic for tool names. I am tracking the matched status of the current `toolName`. When the name changes, I am checking the `toolMappings`. If there\'s a match, I\'ll prefill sensors and parameters from `matchedTool.mappedSensors`, but if no match, I clear the data. The user will then be able to select from `sensors` (the Admin sensors).\n\nI\'ve also been analyzing how parameters will be displayed when there\'s a match. I have now reviewed the `matchedToolMapping.mappedSensors` and the structure of `sensors` from `INITIAL_SENSORS`.\n\n\n**Displaying Parameter Chips**\n\nI\'m now integrating the empty state message and counter. When there\'s no tool name match, the UI will now show, \"No sensors or parameters selected for this tool name. Please select sensors manually from the catalog below.\" The counter will display \"0 of {sensors.length} Sensors Selected \342\200\242 0 Parameters\". Clicking a sensor card in the catalog will activate it, displaying parameter chips for user toggling.\n\nI\'m now checking `DeviceSetup.tsx` to ensure the toolName input field exists. Lines 294-348 show a \"Tool Mapping Profile Section.\"\n\n\n"
